4.1 Decision Structures and the if Statement

• A control structure controls the order in which statements execute.
• A sequence structure is a set of statements that execute in the order they appear.
• A decision structure executes statements only under certain circumstances.
• An if statement executes a block of code when its Boolean expression is true.
• A Boolean expression is an expression that evaluates to either true or false.

```csharp
if (sales > 50000)
{
    bonus = 500;
}
```

Relational Operators

|Operator|Meaning                 |Example |
|--------|------------------------|--------|
|`>`     |Greater than            |`x > y` |
|`<`     |Less than               |`x < y` |
|`>=`    |Greater than or equal to|`x >= y`|
|`<=`    |Less than or equal to   |`x <= y`|
|`==`    |Equal to                |`x == y`|
|`!=`    |Not equal to            |`x != y`|

4.2 The if-else Statement

• An if-else statement has two parts: an if clause and an else clause.
• If the Boolean expression is true, the if block executes.
• If the Boolean expression is false, the else block executes.

```csharp
if (score >= 50)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Pass");
}
else
{
    MessageBox.Show("Fail");
}
```

4.3 Nested Decision Structures

• Nested means one decision structure is placed inside another.
• Nested decision structures are used to test more than one condition.

```csharp
if (salary >= 30000)
{
    if (yearsOnJob >= 2)
    {
        MessageBox.Show("You qualify for the loan");
    }
    else
    {
        MessageBox.Show("Minimum years at current job not met");
    }
}
else
{
    MessageBox.Show("Minimum salary requirement not met");
}
```

The if-else-if Statement

• The if-else-if statement evaluates multiple conditions.
• The first condition that evaluates to true determines which block executes.

```csharp
if (score >= 90)
{
    grade = "A";
}
else if (score >= 80)
{
    grade = "B";
}
else if (score >= 70)
{
    grade = "C";
}
else
{
    grade = "F";
}
```

4.4 Logical Operators

Logical operators connect Boolean expressions to create a compound expression.

|Operator|Meaning|Description                     |
|--------|-------|--------------------------------|
|`&&`    |AND    |Both expressions must be `true`.|
|`              ||`                               |
|`!`     |NOT    |Reverses the Boolean value.     |

```csharp
if (temperature < 20 && minutes > 12)
{
    MessageBox.Show("The temperature is in the danger zone.");
}
```

4.5 bool Variables and Flags

• A bool variable stores either true or false.
• A flag is a variable that signals whether a particular condition exists.
• false indicates that the condition does not exist.
• true indicates that the condition exists.

```csharp
bool isReady = true;

if (isReady)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Ready");
}
```

4.6 Comparing Strings

Strings can be compared using relational operators and methods such as ==, !=, and String.Compare().

```csharp
if (name1 == name2)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Names are the same");
}

if (name1 != "bob")
{
    MessageBox.Show("Name is not bob");
}
```

You can also use String.Compare():

```csharp
if (String.Compare(name1, name2) == 0)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Names are the same");
}
```

4.7 Preventing Data Conversion Exceptions with the TryParse Method

• TryParse methods help prevent exceptions caused by invalid data conversion.
• Examples include int.TryParse, double.TryParse, and decimal.TryParse.
• The out keyword specifies the target variable that receives the converted value.

```csharp
int age;

if (int.TryParse(textBox1.Text, out age))
{
    MessageBox.Show("Valid number");
}
else
{
    MessageBox.Show("Please enter a number");
}
```

4.8 Input Validation

• Input validation is the process of checking entered data to make sure it is valid before the program uses it.
• TryParse checks whether input can be converted to a number, but it does not check whether the value is reasonable.
• For example, a test score should be between 0 and 100.

```csharp
int testScore;

if (int.TryParse(textBox1.Text, out testScore)
    && testScore >= 0 && testScore <= 100)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Valid score");
}
else
{
    MessageBox.Show("Enter a score from 0 to 100");
}
```

4.9 Radio Buttons and Check Boxes

• Radio buttons allow users to select one choice from several options. Selecting one button automatically deselects the others in the same group.
• Check boxes allow users to select multiple options.
• Text holds the text displayed next to the control.
• Checked indicates whether the control is selected (true) or not selected (false).
• The CheckedChanged event occurs when the Checked property changes.

```csharp
if (choiceRadioButton.Checked)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Choice selected");
}
```

```csharp
if (option1CheckBox.Checked)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Option selected");
}
```

4.10 The switch Statement

• The switch statement lets the value of a variable or expression determine which path the program takes.
• It is a multiple-alternative decision structure.
• Each case represents a possible value.
• The break statement ends the current case.
• The optional default section executes when no case matches.

```csharp
switch (day)
{
    case "Monday":
        MessageBox.Show("Start of the week");
        break;
    case "Friday":
        MessageBox.Show("End of the work week");
        break;
    default:
        MessageBox.Show("Another day");
        break;
}
```

4.11 Introduction to List Boxes

• A ListBox displays a list of items and allows the user to select an item.
• Text: Contains the text of the currently selected item.
• Items: Contains the items in the ListBox.
• Items.Count: Contains the number of items.
• SelectedItem: Gets or sets the currently selected item.
• SelectedIndex: Gets or sets the zero-based index of the selected item. If no item is selected, its value is -1.

Adding Items to a ListBox

The Items.Add() method adds an item to a ListBox.

```csharp
daysListBox.Items.Add("Monday");
daysListBox.Items.Add("Tuesday");
daysListBox.Items.Add("Wednesday");
```

Removing Items from a ListBox

The Items.Remove() method removes a specific item.

```csharp
daysListBox.Items.Remove("Tuesday");
```

The Items.RemoveAt() method removes an item at a specific index.

```csharp
daysListBox.Items.RemoveAt(2);
```

The Items.Clear() method removes all items from the ListBox.

```csharp
daysListBox.Items.Clear();
```

Checking Whether an Item Is Selected

A ListBox’s SelectedItem may not contain a selected value. Check SelectedIndex first.

```csharp
if (fruitListBox.SelectedIndex != -1)
{
    string selectedFruit = fruitListBox.SelectedItem.ToString();
    MessageBox.Show(selectedFruit);
}
```